o SD card but then  later we're going to be getting into the  GPIO pins which is really the value of  the Raspberry Pi so you probably should  just go ahead and buy the other things  that we're going to be using so like I  said before the red board will talk more  about how a breadboard works  they might look somewhat different from  this one but generally they're going to  look like this rather they've got a row  here a row here actually officially this  would be more like a column I suppose  anyway something there's something there  and then a bunch of them kind of middle  separated usually but not always and  these will be separated as well so  anyways give a breadboard other than  that we're also going to need quite a  few other things so for example this  these are jumper wires  and these are and try to stay out of my  light there so you can see but these are  male to female or female male jumper  wires you really can't see the pin one  of them has a pin the other one is like  that's the male part and then so for  example this is your male this is your  female and so the male line goes with a  female end so get about at least 10 of  them but usually you can just buy like a  kid or something that has like a hundred  of these things and while you're at it  you could also just get one that has  female to female female to male male the  male just get a bunch of them for this  tutorial series at least as far out as  I've kind of planned um you'll only need  the male the female because basically  the male end goes in the breadboard the  female end goes onto the GPIO pins  themselves and you're good to go so get  about ten of those next we need we're  going to use in LED lights so in this  series we're going to use three we're  going to use a green yellow and red you  don't have to use those colors you can  get different ones it doesn't really  matter but get some LED lights you  should probably get more than three  because not always do they work and  they're just a 